Fascinating [study from the BMJ on fall reports](https://www.bmj.com/content/337/bmj.a2227.full). Here's a summary, and the full results and conclusions below. Interestingly, the anecdotal stories I have of my grandfather, uncle and friend's grandmother aren't isolated incidents - they're common among the "oldest old" and can lead (as I found out) to "serious consequences". More interestingly, it recommends three ways to improve these outcomes (and this was back in 2008):
1. training strategies to get off the floor
2. access and activation issues of call alarms and info on their use
3. care providers better understand of perceptions of older people.

A key quote is that "Lying on the floor for a long time is thus one of the most serios conditions of a fall..." and as the population ages this problem is going to get worse and worse. 

Almost 20 years ago, this research found that despite call alarm systems being increasingly available and effective, "uptake and adherence is low". 

![

As I mentioned earlier, over half of the study's fall reports were found on the floor, with 88% along when this happened, and the inability to get up off the floor is common. 

Many of them in the study had call alarm systems, usually a pendant or call bells. But - and this is fascinated - in 80% of cases they didn't use it to summon help, despite being on the floor in some cases for over and hour.

And why didn't they use it?
1. Not having/wanting a call alarm - anecdotal evidence: "she thinks it would keep going off by mistake"
2. Having one but not wearing it - they don't like to wear it, or take it off for bed.
3. Wearing but choosing not to use it - not wanting to cause a fuss, not liking to have to call for help, fear of having to go to hospital
4. Difficulty in activating the alarm - couldn't reach it or the pendant didn't work.

It recommends studies on older people's views on assistive tech devices, there's evidence either way of generating more confidence but also reluctance to seek or accept help. 

I'm just going to quote their full results:

> **Results** In one year’s intensive follow-up, 54% (144/265) of fall reports described the participant as being found on the floor and 82% (217/265) of falls occurred when the person was alone. Of the 60% who fell, 80% (53/66) were unable to get up after at least one fall and 30% (20/66) had lain on the floor for an hour or more. Difficulty in getting up was consistently associated with age, reported mobility, and severe cognitive impairment. Cognition was the only characteristic that predicted lying on the floor for a long time. Lying on the floor for a long time was strongly associated with serious injuries, admission to hospital, and subsequent moves into long term care. Call alarms were widely available but were not used in most cases of falls that led to lying on the floor for a long time. Comments from older people and carers showed the complexity of issues around the use of call alarms, including perceptions of irrelevance, concerns about independence, and practical difficulties.
> **Conclusions** Lying on the floor for a long time after falling is more common among the “oldest old” than previously thought and is associated with serious consequences. Factors indicating higher risk and comments from participants suggest practical implications. People need training in strategies to get up from the floor. Work is needed on access and activation issues for design of call alarms and information for their effective use. Care providers need better understanding of the perceptions of older people to provide acceptable support services.

54% of 265 incidents recorded said the patient was found on the floor and 82% of them occured when the person was alone. 
